1404	`sys.argv()` is the list of arguments after the program path, and it is empty
  1405	for a program run from the REPL or from a test, because only `rb run` collects
  1406	them.
  1407	
  1408	### time
  1409	
  1410	```redblue
  1411	set stamp to time.now()                  // a record: seconds, nanoseconds
  1412	time.sleep(0.5)                          // pause for half a second
  1413	say time.format(1704067200, "%Y-%m-%d")  // "2024-01-01"
  1414	say time.unix("2024-01-01 00:00:00")     // 1704067200
  1415	say time.from_unix(1704067200)           // 2024-01-01 00:00:00
  1416	```
  1417	
  1418	`time.now()` is a record of `seconds` and `nanoseconds` since
  1419	1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and `time.sleep` answers `nothing` once the pause is over.
  1420	`time.format` takes a number of seconds since the same instant and a format
  1421	string; with no format string it writes `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`. `time.unix` is the
  1422	other direction: it takes `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` and answers the seconds since
  1423	the same instant, read as UTC wherever it runs; `time.from_unix(seconds)` is the
  1424	inverse and turns such seconds into a date value, read as UTC too.
  1425	
  1426	`time.sleep` is the one call in this section that blocks — the other three are
  1427	computed and returned immediately, so `time.format` and `time.unix` cost no wall
  1428	time whatever the clock says. A program that wants a timer rather than a pause
  1429	has to read the clock itself — `time.now()` twice and `time.sleep` between them.
  1430	The calls that block besides `time.sleep` are `network.get` and `network.post`,
  1431	which wait for a response.
  1432	
  1433	#### Dates and durations
  1434	
  1435	A **date** is a first-class value: it can be stored in a record or a list,
  1436	passed to and returned from a function, compared, and printed. A **duration** is
  1437	a signed span of time, and it is a first-class value too.
  1438	
  1439	```redblue
  1440	set start to time.datetime(2024, 2, 29, 23, 30, 0)
  1441	set next  to time.add_months(start, 1)      // 2024-03-29 23:30:00
  1442	set span  to next - start                   // a duration of 29 days
  1443	say to_text(span)                           // 29 days
  1444	
  1445	set later to start + time.duration(86400)   // exactly one day later
  1446	say later                                   // 2024-03-01 23:30:00
  1447	
  1448	set jan to time.date(2024, 1, 31)
  1449	say to_text(time.add_months(jan, 1))        // 2024-02-29 00:00:00
  1450	say to_text(time.add_years(jan, 1))         // 2025-01-31 00:00:00
  1451	say to_text(time.add_days(jan, 1))          // 2024-02-01 00:00:00
  1452	say to_text(time.add_hours(jan, 24))        // 2024-02-01 00:00:00
  1453	say to_text(time.add_minutes(jan, 60))      // 2024-01-31 01:00:00
  1454	say to_text(time.add_seconds(jan, 1))       // 2024-01-31 00:00:01
  1455	```
  1456	
  1457	`time.date(year, month, day)` makes a date at midnight, and
  1458	`time.datetime(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)` makes one at the stated
  1459	time. Both read whole numbers and refuse a component that is not a real part of
  1460	the calendar (`2024-02-30`, month `13`, hour `24`) with a catchable runtime
  1461	error naming the builtin. `time.duration(seconds)` makes a duration from a
  1462	number of seconds; the number may be negative or fractional (to millisecond
  1463	precision).
  1464	
  1465	The operators are defined on these values:
  1466	
  1467	| Expression | Answer |
  1468	|---|---|
  1469	| `date < date`, `date <= date`, `date > date`, `date >= date` | `yes` or `no` |
  1470	| `date is date` | `yes` when the two readings are identical |
  1471	| `date - date` | a duration, negative when the left date is earlier |
  1472	| `date + duration`, `duration + date` | the date moved by that span |
  1473	| `date - duration` | the date moved back by that span |
  1474	| `duration + duration`, `duration - duration` | a duration |
  1475	| `duration < duration`, and the other orderings | `yes` or `no` |
  1476	
  1477	A date or a duration compared with a number is a **runtime error**, not a
  1478	coercion, whether the comparison is an ordering or `is`/`is not`:
  1479	`time.date(2024, 1, 1) < 5` and `time.date(2024, 1, 1) is 5` both name the two
  1480	kinds they mixed, and `try … catch error of Runtime` can catch either. Adding a
  1481	date to a date, or subtracting a date from a duration, is refused the same way.
  1482	
  1483	The components of a date are read as properties: `year`, `month`, `day`, `hour`,
  1484	`minute`, `second`, and `weekday` (Monday is `1`, Sunday is `7`). The components
  1485	of a duration are `days`, `hours`, `minutes`, `seconds` (the remainder inside
  1486	the last day, hour and minute), `total_seconds`, and `nanoseconds`. A negative
  1487	span carries its sign in each component, so `time.duration(-1.5).seconds` is
  1488	`-1` and its `nanoseconds` is `-500000000`; the components sum to
  1489	`total_seconds`. An unknown property answers `nothing`, as a missing record
  1490	field does.
  1491	
  1492	Calendar arithmetic is `time.add_years`, `time.add_months`, `time.add_days`,
  1493	`time.add_hours`, `time.add_minutes` and `time.add_seconds`; each takes a date
  1494	and a whole number, and a negative number moves the date backwards. Years and
  1495	months are calendar units, not fixed spans:
  1496	
  1497	- **Month-end overflow clamps to the last day of the target month.**
  1498	  `time.add_months(time.date(2024, 1, 31), 1)` is `2024-02-29`; the same call in
  1499	  2023 is `2023-02-28`; and `time.add_months(time.date(2024, 3, 31), -1)` is
  1500	  `2024-02-29`.
  1501	- **29 February plus one year is 28 February.** `time.add_years` clamps the same
  1502	  way, so `time.add_years(time.date(2024, 2, 29), 1)` is `2025-02-28`.
  1503	- Days, hours, minutes and seconds are fixed spans: one day is always 24 hours
  1504	  and one hour is always 60 minutes. A move that leaves the range of dates the
  1505	  implementation can represent is a runtime error naming the builtin.
  1506	
  1507	**Timezone stance.** A date is *timezone-naive*: it has no zone, no offset and
  1508	no daylight-saving rule. The components are the same reading everywhere, so
  1509	`2024-03-10` is not a 23-hour day and `2024-11-03` is not a 25-hour day — adding
  1510	24 hours always lands on the same clock time on the next calendar day. Programs
  1511	that need a zone, an offset, or a conversion to local time must carry that
  1512	information themselves; this phase deliberately defines the zone-free value
  1513	first. `time.format` and `time.unix` continue to read their seconds as UTC and
  1514	are unchanged.
  1515	
  1516	`time.format` does not yet format a date value, and there is no parsing from a
  1517	date text; those belong to the next phase. A date can be printed with `say` or
  1518	`to_text`, which writes `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`.
  1519	
  1520	### regex
  1521	
  1522	```redblue
  1523	say regex.match("id=42", "id=([0-9]+)")      // {text: id=42, ...}
  1524	say regex.is_match("id=42", "^id")           // yes
  1525	say regex.capture("id=42", "id=([0-9]+)", 1) // 42
